           <p>
            Use Proposition 14.4.9 for 4.3 and 4.4
           </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>R</m>  be a ring and let <m>M</m>, <m>N</m>  be two isomorphic <m> R</m> -modules.
              Let <m> U\subset M</m>  be a submodule of <m>M</m>  and <m> V\subset N</m>  be a submodule of
             <m> N</m>  such that <m> U\cong V</m>. 
Let  <m> f\colon M\to N</m>   and <m> g\colon U\to V</m>  
    be <m> R</m> -module isomorphisms such  that <m> g(p)=f(p)</m>  for all <m> p\in U</m>.
         Prove that the following hold: 
    <ol>
 <li>
    <p>If <m> m\in M</m> , then <m> f(m)\in V</m>  if and only if <m> m\in U</m> .
    </p>
    </li> 

<li>
    <p> <m> M/U\cong N/V</m> . 
    </p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
    <p>
 Construct first a homomorphism <m> M\to N/V</m>  -- its definition will probably depend on <m> f</m> . Show it is onto. Then use the First isomorphism theorem.       </p>
    </hint>
    </exercise>

<solution>
    <p>
        Remark: In the problem above: If <m> M\cong N</m>  and <m> U\cong V</m> , it is not guaranteed that we can construct isomorphisms <m> f,g</m>  as above, satisfying <m> g(p)=f(p)</m>  for all <m> p\in U</m> . If this condition is not satisfied, then the conlcusion <m> M/U\cong N/V</m>  in part (2) may not hold. 

We want to give an example in the problem below. To make it easy, let's assume that <m> M=N</m> . 
    </p>
</solution>

   
<exercise>
<statement>
    <p>
      Give an example of a  ring <m> R</m> , an <m> R</m> -module <m> M</m>  and two submodules <m> U</m> , <m> V</m>  of <m> M</m>  such that <m> U\cong V</m> , but <m> M/U\not\cong M/V</m> .   
    </p>
  
</statement>
<hint>
    <p>
    How do you prove that two modules are NOT isomorphic? This idea may work sometimes: In view of a problem from the last homework, if  <m>\Ann(A)\ne \Ann(B)</m> , you can conclude right away that <m> A\not\cong B</m>.
    </p>
</hint>
</exercise>

</exercises>
</section>